## 2. Hotspot Analysis

**This is the most important section in this document.** Before starting a round, the Optimizer reads this section to understand not just which hotspot was picked, but how you reasoned about the whole performance picture — so it can sanity-check your call, notice if you missed something, and pick up context fast without re-deriving it from the raw trace. A thin or table-only write-up here hands the Optimizer a worse understanding than you actually have. Write at whatever length that requires: a few hundred words per hotspot is normal, and a few thousand across all of them is fine — there is no length ceiling here. Do not compress this into a checklist.

**Work on the utilization timeline: a hotspot is a low-utilization window.** The method, in order:

1. **Walk the compute stream's `stream_<id>/util.json`** (the stream with the highest `busy_ms`) bucket by bucket, and mark every contiguous stretch of low-util buckets (e.g. util < 0.5 across several consecutive buckets).
2. **Cross-check the same buckets in `gpu_util.json`** (device level, union across ALL streams). A compute-stream gap that another stream fills is overlap, not waste — only windows that are low at the **device** level count as true dead time.
3. **Quantify each true window** — bucket range, duration, idle ms, % of step — then open those buckets in `timeline_bucket.json` (`by_source`, kernel counts, average kernel size, `kernel_ms` vs `memcpy_ms`) and read the code to explain **why** the GPU is starved there.
4. **Rank windows by idle mass.** Beware self-time numbers: a big `sync.json` row whose host wait overlaps GPU-busy work costs the step nothing — the timeline, not an op's own duration, decides what matters.

A short table for orientation (a supplement to the narrative below, not a replacement for it):

| rank | window (buckets) | idle | device-level util | suspected cause |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | <e.g. 31–45 (15 buckets)> | <e.g. 132 ms, 13% of step> | <e.g. 0.03–0.31> | <one line> |
| 2 | ... | ... | ... | ... |

Then, **for every window, in rank order, a full narrative subsection covering all three of what / why / evidence** — don't skip "why": a description of a window without its root cause is only half the analysis. List each distinct cause you can see here; §3 groups the same-cause ones into clusters and §4 picks one.

### Hotspot 1 — <short name>

**What it is.** Describe the behavior/pattern observed — where in the step it falls, which stream or thread, what kind of cost it is (compute, sync, launch, memory traffic). Write enough that someone who hasn't looked at the trace yet can picture it. Example: "During backward, the compute stream goes idle for roughly 22% of the step, from bucket 40 through bucket 55 of `stream_0/timeline_bucket.json`. The gap sits between the embedding-gradient scatter and the dense-optimizer step, and it recurs identically on every one of the profiled steps."

**Why it happens.** This is usually the part worth the most words. Don't stop at "there's a sync here" — explain *why* the code produces it: an algorithmic reason (e.g. a data-dependent shape forces a host read), a framework behavior (e.g. a `.item()` call inside a Python loop, or an implicit `cudaStreamSynchronize` from a CPU-side conditional on a GPU value), an architectural one (e.g. per-expert dispatch with no batching, or a collective that can't overlap with this particular compute shape). This is what tells the Optimizer whether there's a real fix available or only a surface symptom of something deeper. State the root cause itself (the code / behavior reason); **do not name a technique, op, API, or flag to use, and do not describe the remedy** — that is the Optimizer's call. Example: "`comm/model.py:118` reads `num_active.item()` to size a subsequent gather, which forces a host round-trip on every step. This exists because the number of active experts is only known after the router runs, so the original implementation blocks on it."

**Evidence.** Specific citations, not impressions — file:line, the workspace file and field, timeline bucket index, ms figures, percentage of step, kernel counts. Example: "`sync.json`: `comm/model.py:118` accounts for 4.8ms/step (22% of the 21.8ms typical step). `stream_0/util.json` shows buckets 40–55 idle (util < 0.05) over the identical window. `veloq slices` over that window shows a `cudaStreamSynchronize` immediately preceded by the `.item()` call, with no overlapping work issued on the comm or copy streams in the same range."

